{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Potassium nitrate is used as an electrolyte in the salt bridge of a copper-zinc cell. Which ONE of the following CORRECTLY shows the direction of migration of potassium and nitrate ions in the cell?\n\n| | POTASSIUM IONS TO THE: | NITRATE IONS TO THE: |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | Anode | Cathode |\n| B | Negative electrode | Positive electrode |\n| C | Zinc electrode | Copper electrode |\n| D | Copper electrode | Zinc electrode |"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner is blank-stuck, so begin with a single recall question rather than naming the ion directions. Identifying the anode will establish the electrode labels needed to reason about the salt bridge.",
      "content": "Let’s start with just one clue: in a copper-zinc cell, which metal electrode is the **anode**—zinc or copper?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm not sure. Maybe copper? I thought potassium ions might go to the zinc side."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has made an attempt but has reversed the electrode roles and ion directions. Correct this gently: zinc is the anode, where oxidation adds Zn²⁺ to solution; nitrate ions move there to balance excess positive charge. At the copper cathode, Cu²⁺ ions are removed from solution, so K⁺ ions move there. Then state the memo answer and connect it to the general salt-bridge rule.",
      "content": "You’re considering the right idea—that the salt bridge ions move to balance charge—but the electrode labels are reversed. In a copper-zinc cell, **zinc is the anode** and **copper is the cathode**.\n\nAt the zinc anode, zinc forms Zn²⁺ ions, so negative nitrate ions migrate towards the zinc side. At the copper cathode, Cu²⁺ ions are removed from the solution, so positive potassium ions migrate towards the copper side.\n\n**Final answer: D — potassium ions migrate to the copper electrode, and nitrate ions migrate to the zinc electrode.**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** \\(\\text{cations} \\to \\text{cathode},\\quad \\text{anions} \\to \\text{anode}\\)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * \\(\\mathrm{K^+}\\): the positive potassium ion, which is a cation.\n  * \\(\\mathrm{NO_3^-}\\): the negative nitrate ion, which is an anion.\n* **Why we use it:** Ions in the salt bridge move to keep each half-cell electrically balanced. In this cell, \\(\\mathrm{K^+}\\) goes to the copper cathode and \\(\\mathrm{NO_3^-}\\) goes to the zinc anode.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Identify the anode and cathode first, then apply the rule for cations and anions. In a galvanic copper-zinc cell, zinc is the negative anode and copper is the positive cathode."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "socratic_hints",
    "final_answer": "D",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nanswer = sp.Symbol(\"D\")\nprint(answer)",
    "techniques": [
      "check_by_substitution",
      "units_check"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "messages": [
